Thank you, Chair. I note the statements of the briefers.
Colleagues, the world today does not lack for crises. It is unfortunate, with so many pressing issues before us, that the Russian Federation has chosen to use another Arria-formula meeting to distract attention from the core issues of peace and security – including Moscow’s decision to continue the Russia-Ukraine war.
A discussion of communications satellites is better suited for other fora, not the UN Security Council. For the Council to be effective, it should focus on urgent threats to international peace and security – not least the ongoing war between Russia and Ukraine. When it comes to outer space policy, Russia should focus on ensuring its own activities meet its obligations under the Outer Space Treaty. Russia’s plans to place a nuclear weapon in orbit as an anti-satellite capability would harm all countries’ national security, as well as commercial satellites and infrastructure.
When it comes to international peace and security, Russia should focus on ending its war with Ukraine, rather than attempting to use meetings like this as a distraction. We continue to urge Russia to negotiate a lasting peace in good faith and accept an immediate and comprehensive ceasefire.
If Russia objects to Ukraine’s use of satellite communications to coordinate military operations in its self-defense against Russia’s invading forces and guide UAVs against military targets in Russia, then Russia should simply end the war. Russia can do so any time it chooses by agreeing to a ceasefire, as Ukraine has already demonstrated it is ready to do, and entering direct negotiations on a lasting end to the war.
As to the issues Russia has raised today, the United States emphasizes that commercial satellites, including large satellite constellations, provide critical benefits including disaster preparedness and recovery, search and rescue capabilities, and reliable communications for communities with limited alternative means of Internet access. The United States has consistently maintained that activities of commercial entities under the authorization and continuing supervision of states parties to the Outer Space Treaty are to be carried out in conformity with the provisions thereof.
Thank you.
