UNITED STATES MISSION TO THE UNITED NATIONS
Office of Press and Public Diplomacy
FOR IMMEDIATE RELEASE
Ambassador Tammy Bruce Confirmed as Deputy U.S. Representative to the United Nations
NEW YORK — Today, following confirmation by the United States Senate, Ambassador Tammy Bruce was sworn in to serve as Ambassador Extraordinary and Plenipotentiary, Deputy Representative of the United States of America to the United Nations. Ambassador Bruce’s confirmation completes the senior leadership team appointed by President Donald J. Trump to advance his America First foreign policy agenda and pursue comprehensive reform at the global body.
Serving as Ambassador Mike Waltz’s principal deputy, Ambassador Bruce joins a distinguished team at the U.S. Mission to the United Nations. The leadership cabinet also includes Ambassador Jennifer Locetta (Alternative Representative for Special Political Affairs), Ambassador Jeff Bartos (Representative for Management and Reform), and Ambassador Dan Negrea (Representative for Economic and Social Council).
“Ambassador Bruce is a fierce advocate for American interests and a principled voice for the values that define our nation,” said Ambassador Mike Waltz. “Her arrival marks a pivotal moment for our mission. With our full leadership team now in place, we are ready to take bold action to ensure the United Nations returns to its founding mission while remaining accountable to the American taxpayers.”
Prior to her confirmation, Ambassador Bruce served as Spokesperson for the U.S. Department of State and as the White House Public Delegate to the U.S. Mission to the United Nations. She has also led nonprofit organizations focused on advocacy for women and is a best-selling author and former commentator. Together, these roles bring decades of experience navigating complex political and media landscapes to the U.S. Mission.
