Ambassador Jeffrey Bartos
U.S. Representative for U.N. Management and Reform
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Mr. President, for convening this meeting on Resolution 2231. And thank you to Under-Secretary-General DiCarlo for your briefing.
This Council is and should remain seized of the nonproliferation issue, especially with respect to the Islamic Republic of Iran.
It is clear – as demonstrated by the issuance of the Secretary-General’s recent report on Resolution 2231 – that, with the exception of paragraphs 7, 8, and 16 to 20, this resolution and its related practical arrangements remain in effect.
Additionally, pursuant to the snapback process outlined in Resolution 2231, the Council decided to reinstate Resolutions 1696, 1737, 1747, 1803, 1835, and 1929.
These resolutions are not arbitrary or punitive but rather narrowly scoped to address a nuclear program that seeks to operate out of view of the international community – and in continued non-compliance with its Nuclear Nonproliferation Treaty (NPT)-mandated comprehensive safeguards agreement with the IAEA, as reaffirmed last month by the IAEA Board.
The reimposition of these proliferation-focused measures reflects the Council’s longstanding concern – reaffirmed in its September 19 vote to restore these resolutions – about the Iranian nuclear file.
As noted on the UN website, the 1737 Committee was immediately reestablished concurrent with the reinstatement of these resolutions.
The United States will continue to work to ensure this committee is a fully functioning, active body.
The United States continues to prefer a negotiated solution to this matter.
Absent that solution, the Council must operationalize the 1737 Committee.
Moreover, these resolutions, if implemented by Member States in good faith, can constrain the space for potential escalation and uncertainty.
The United States therefore urges its fellow members to support the expeditious implementation of the reinstated UN Security Council Resolutions, to realize their potential as a bulwark against insecurity.
Thank you.
