U.S. Statement at the Humanitarian Debate of the UN General Assembly, Presenting Amendments to the Resolution Titled “Strengthening of the Coordination of Emergency Humanitarian Assistance of the United Nations.”
Ambassador Jennifer Locetta
Alternative Representative for Special Political Affairs
New York, New York
AS PREPARED
Thank you, Madam President,
The United States submitted our amendments in good faith. We have also received support from other Member States, which is why we respectfully refuse to withdraw these amendments.
The United States has introduced these amendments to protect the original purpose of this resolution from ideological agendas, just like the advancement of humanitarian assistance and the promotion of international humanitarian law. Specifically, these amendments address inappropriate, divisive, and harmful references to sexual and reproductive health, rights to abortion, and climate change that will undermine the purpose of this resolution. We should be looking at women’s health more holistically and not breaking out specific elements. Humanitarian settings and conflicts require access to health care, thus if we start looking at specific elements of health, like SRH, we would miss other key elements. For these reasons we prefer to make the language more general.
The United States rejects terminology around “sexual and reproductive healthcare.” This language is unnecessary, and also carries highly controversial meanings, conflating this term with notions of abortion, LGBTQ, and other sexual rights. “Sexual and reproductive health” is also a broad category that not all can be considered a “humanitarian need.” It also includes many controversial health services. We stress in this regard that there is no international “right to abortion,” whether under that name or other terms, and there is no duty on the part of the United States to finance or facilitate abortions.
The United States believes in legal protections for the unborn. Consistent with the Geneva Consensus Declaration, signed by countries representing every region of the world, we assert that each nation has the sovereign right to legislate against abortion absent external pressure, especially from the United Nations.
Accordingly, the U.S. proposed amendments to OP66 and OP67 are necessary to limit the reference to “sexual and reproductive health” to language on which there is international consensus, namely, the Program of Action of International Conference on Population and Development and its review outcomes as adopted by the General Assembly.
The United States has also offered its amendment on OP67 to clarify and stress that there is no international “right to abortion,” whether under that name or under any other term, nor is there any duty on part of the United States to finance or facilitate abortions. Instead, humanitarian response efforts should include every attempt to eliminate the need for abortion and provide women with the best possible options for family planning, maternal healthcare, psychological support and other essential services.
Furthermore, President Trump has delivered a very clear message: the United Nations and so many other nations of the world have gone wildly off track, exaggerating climate change into the world’s greatest threat. This practice should no longer be tolerated by this body.
The United States has therefore offered additional critical amendments to PPs 4 and 15 to address the divisive and inappropriate references to climate change in this resolution.
We urge delegations to recognize the importance of upholding real humanitarianism, protecting it from infiltration by radical and ideologically-driven actors by supporting these critical elements.
Thank you.
