Ambassador Dan Negrea
Representative of the United States to the UN Economic and Social Council
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
The United States opposes the absurdity of International Days for pet causes and completely rejects the entire unilateral coercive measures – or UCM – narrative devoted to discrediting sanctions as a tool of statecraft.
Economic sanctions can be a legitimate and effective tool for responding to destabilizing activity and addressing threats to peace and security. The United States is not alone in that view or in that practice.
In cases where the United States has applied sanctions, we have done so consistent with international law and with specific objectives in mind, including as a means to respond to threats to international security.
Sponsors of this event – including Venezuela, Cuba, Iran, the DPRK, and Russia – are stoking an incorrect narrative while engaging in some of the most abhorrent and destabilizing activities of our time. Ironically, many of these states also either use sanctions themselves or cooperate with those who do, rendering this entire exercise performative.
We will continue to use sanctions to curb their ability to threaten their neighbors, repress their nationals, pursue nuclear weapons, sponsor terrorism, and facilitate drug trafficking and other illicit activity.
Nothing that happens here infringes upon the sovereign rights of the United States to sanction those countries worthy of sanctioning. UN officials should also refrain from wading into the sovereign prerogatives of Member States.
Further, we will continue to use sanctions to advance our interests and target those who seek to undermine them.
It is outrageous that the UN is being used as a platform to denigrate our sanctions rather than make UN sanctions a more effective, credible tool to address threats to international peace and security.
The sponsors of this event are doing everything they can to undermine UN sanctions, too, whether through blatant noncompliance or by threatening or wielding vetoes to erode mandates and block updates to UN sanctions lists.
To the many states who voted to support the establishment of this day, we ask that you re-examine your stance on this issue and stop lending it credibility.
The value of the UN is first and foremost to maintain peace and security, and this institution cannot do so effectively without stronger and more effective UN sanctions.
We should be talking about deficits in the UN’s ability to manage threats, and what we can do collectively to ensure the UN uses scarce resources to get “Back to Basics.”
In this vein, the United States categorically rejects the mandate of the Special Rapporteur on Unilateral Coercive Measures. Just like this day, this mandate is completely inappropriate and is a waste of valuable time and resources. Member States should focus on advancing economic development and stability rather than perpetuating false narratives that hinder progress.
We will be looking in the new year to advance these objectives and invite you to work with us to address deficiencies in UN sanctions and ensure those measures are the focus of attention in UN fora.
