Ambassador Jeff Bartos
U.S. Representative for U.N. Management and Reform
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Before I begin my remarks, I just want to thank the co-chairs for your leadership and for the time you’ve devoted to this process, the result of which are these very thoughtful remarks from all of the delegations and all of the groups that weve heard over the last few working sessions and again this morning. So a sincere thank you again for your leadership and good morning Under Secretary General Ryder.
Colleagues, with finite resources and an ever-growing set of demands, we—Member States—have a responsibility to ensure that the UN’s work and its budget remain tightly aligned with our highest priorities. Every program, every mandate, every line of the budget should be judged against one simple test: is it delivering the results we expect, and is it still needed?
Far too often, our answer is simply assumed rather than examined. Many mandates roll over year after year, often with minimal debate and even less adjustment. We seldom ask whether they remain fit for purpose, whether they have achieved the task we set, or whether our collective time is being used in the most strategic way. That approach is neither sustainable nor responsible. Mandates should include sunset clauses—or at the very least explicit review dates—so we can ensure they evolve, consolidate, or conclude based on evidence, not inertia.
We support a more collective, structured assessment of categories or groups of mandates. The Secretary-General should bring forward to the General Assembly a list of deliverables that are ripe for consolidation or, where obsolete or ineffective, for termination. This is not about reducing ambition; it is about sharpening it. A system with clearer priorities and fewer duplicative tasks will be more impactful, more coherent, and more credible.
The same discipline is needed across the programs of work of the General Assembly, ECOSOC, and other bodies. Streamlining agenda items, consolidating overlapping discussions, and eliminating replication and overlap across bodies would free up resources—human and financial—to focus on areas where the UN can actually move the needle.
The United Nations uses the phrase “results-based management,” but too often it is focused on outputs like the number of meetings, reports, trainings, or patrols—rather than outcomes that reflect real-world impact. Without a clear link between activities, resources, and results, the UN cannot demonstrate its achievements or its value.
We strongly support harmonizing the tools that the UN uses to build and track results chains. Consistent standards, clearer indicators, and transparent reporting would allow Member States to understand not only what is being done, but whether it works.
If we are serious about impact, then we must be serious about accountability, prioritization, and reform. This is how we ensure that every mandate, every program, and every dollar serves the people the UN was created to help.
Thank you.
