Ambassador Jeff Bartos
U.S. Representative for U.N. Management and Reform
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Madam Chair, Mr. Secretary-General, Colleagues,
The Secretary-General’s UN80 Initiative and his proposed revised estimates are a necessary first step for the United Nations to get back to basics, to renew its founding mission to maintain international peace and security, and to rebuild trust across the world that the UN can be a responsible steward of the precious resources that Member States entrust to it.
As part of UN80, the Secretary-General proposed a 15 percent budget cut and the elimination of 2600 posts. As the Fifth Committee considers this first step in our wider reform mission, we cannot, we must not, backtrack in any way from the Secretary-General’s revised estimates.
The United States regards these necessary reductions as permanent, not temporary fixes. For this overdue reform is about more than short-term savings; it is about creating a focused organization capable of achieving measurable results without endlessly expanding its footprint.
The time for endless talk about the need for reform, meetings about meetings, and tapping the world along with unserious half-measures of reform, is over. The United States will not shy away from the decisions needed to ensure this institution is leaner, more focused, and more effective in achieving its foundational mission of maintaining international peace and security while respecting the national sovereignty of Member States.
We look forward to working together during the last month of this Main Session to achieve these essential, durable and consequential reforms.
Thank you.
